From: ggbdpq <ggbdpq@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 02:37:34 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] gateway: the ChatGPT backend's bare 400 to a big request is
 read as its byte cap (#6305)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

chatgpt.com's Codex backend refuses a request body of about 880 KB and
more with a bare 400, its body empty, and says nothing of a cap
(guanwenpeng2001-bot, #6214, where the ceiling was measured at about
130 K tokens of real traffic — a thread past it is refused on every
turn, and Codex's own compaction, triggered on tokens a third of the
window in, never comes). The same bytes as random text reach the model
check and are refused with a word (adlternative's control, #6305), so
the refusal is of the body, before any of the API's own errors are
said. byteCapRefusal never matched it: no 413, no words — the request
failed as "Codex: Bad Request" and every turn of the thread after it
died the same way.

A bare 400 to a request of 768 KB or more, its body empty, is now read
as that byte cap (bareCapRefusal), the way a proxy's page for a big
request already is (pageOverSize): the cap it implies is a guess at
one, not the vendor's word, and is kept for no later request; the
oldest images are left out and the request asked again (a thread of
screenshots fits under it and goes on), and when nothing more can go
the refusal is said as a 413 that names the size — Codex's cue to
compact, which the thread never had. A bare 400 with words in it,
however big the request, and a small request's bare 400 stay what they
were.

Run: TestBigBodyBare400IsByteCap sends a text turn past the threshold
to an upstream that answers it with a bare 400: refused with no
details on the base, said as a 413 naming the size with the fix, the
upstream asked once. TestBare400NotAByteCap keeps a bare 400 with
words and a small request's bare 400 as they were (green before the
fix, kept as guards). The body-cap family, the gateway package, gofmt
and vet stay green.

